\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Bootstrap 95\% confidence intervals for every cell of Table~\ref {tab:h2h}.} $^{a}$The 1.5B-blind bootstrap resamples are degenerate (concentrated scores), so its conviction carries jackknife and permutation inference instead: the bracketed interval is the jackknife CI, and the permutation test gives $p = 0.0017$ (two-sided, $n{=}53$). $^{b}$The reward model's inflation is $+1.46\sigma $ $[+1.33, +1.58]$ under the candidate-side paraphrase replicate (Section~\ref {sec:limits}), an analogous but not identical estimand to the judge protocol's. Inflation intervals reported in the paper appear in Table~\ref {tab:robust}, panel E; the remaining per-configuration inflation intervals are in the repository analysis output (Section~\ref {sec:setup}, Reproducibility).}}{18}{table.caption.36}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:h2h_ci}{{2}{18}{\textbf {Bootstrap 95\% confidence intervals for every cell of Table~\ref {tab:h2h}.} $^{a}$The 1.5B-blind bootstrap resamples are degenerate (concentrated scores), so its conviction carries jackknife and permutation inference instead: the bracketed interval is the jackknife CI, and the permutation test gives $p = 0.0017$ (two-sided, $n{=}53$). $^{b}$The reward model's inflation is $+1.46\sigma $ $[+1.33, +1.58]$ under the candidate-side paraphrase replicate (Section~\ref {sec:limits}), an analogous but not identical estimand to the judge protocol's. Inflation intervals reported in the paper appear in Table~\ref {tab:robust}, panel E; the remaining per-configuration inflation intervals are in the repository analysis output (Section~\ref {sec:setup}, Reproducibility)}{table.caption.36}{}}
